**Read:** [Radiative Transfer — MIT 2.58J](../sources/Radiative-Transfer-MIT-2-58J.lingua#r_0SGHEYZJFKN0ZNS3AYJ39XPT86), the complete near-field and fluctuation-dissipation lectures; [Nanoscale thermal transport II — Cahill and colleagues](../sources/Nanoscale-Thermal-Transport-II-Cahill.lingua#r_30EZYRYJ20TDTWN3JXWD9KWSFG), nanoscale-gap transport and experimental limitations; [Electromagnetism — charge, fields, matter, waves and physical evidence](../../electromagnetism/Electromagnetism.lingua#r_5TJPESCTTNAKY66JH2QNQYN1PY), the relevant wave/material and evanescent-field explanations.

At separations comparable to relevant electromagnetic decay and wavelength scales, evanescent fields and material response can change thermal exchange beyond a far-field ray model. Define gap, geometry, temperature, optical properties and the assumptions of the fluctuation/response formulation.

Study the complete graduate derivation and compare its far-field or large-gap limit with radiative transfer. Distinguish a spectral enhancement per area from the total useful service and from nonradiative contact or gas paths in an experiment.

Trace one complete primary measurement or calculation through surface preparation, gap knowledge, electromagnetic model and uncertainty. Do not use the breakdown of a grey far-field formula as evidence that energy or thermodynamic accounting has failed. The microscopic and electromagnetic descriptions must close the same physical energy balance.

